<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Berlin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was the western part of Berlin, formed from the American, British and French occupation sectors of the city, an enclave surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> from 1949 to 1990. It survived the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of 1948–1949 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a> and was closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, though formally under Allied authority. From 1961 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> cut it off from East Berlin until the Wall fell in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-berlin.jpg" alt="West Berlin" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flaggenentwurf: unbekannt diese Datei: Jwnabd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Berlin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The London Protocol of 1944 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a> set the framework for the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after the war. Germany was to be administered by four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and divided into four zones. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, surrounded by the Soviet zone, was divided likewise, with the Western Allies occupying an enclave of its western parts. The occupation of Berlin could end only by a quadripartite agreement, and the Western Allies were guaranteed three air corridors to their sectors. West Berlin consisted of the American, British and French sectors established in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was established on 23 May 1949, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> remained occupied territory under the formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, so the Basic Law was not fully applicable to it. West Berlin nonetheless aligned itself politically with the Federal Republic, and most of its residents were West German citizens. It was heavily subsidised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> as a showcase of the West. West Berliners could travel freely except during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Blockade</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, from 24 June 1948 to 12 May 1949, when the airlift limited flights. Road and rail travel to West Germany passed through East German border checks along transit routes, and air traffic under Allied control was the only link not directly under East German control.</p><p>On 27 May 1952 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> closed its border with West Berlin, and West Berliners then needed a permit to enter the East. Movement between the two halves of the city remained free for more than a decade until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> was built in 1961. After its construction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> suggested that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> propose to swap West Berlin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Mecklenburg; the Kennedy administration considered the idea but did not make the offer. Three underground and S-Bahn lines ran through tunnels beneath East Berlin, with their eastern stations shut and empty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> had about two million inhabitants, the largest population of any city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, and was noted for its cosmopolitan character and as a centre of education, research and culture. It had its own postal administration, issuing its own stamps, though in practice its post and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephones</a> were integrated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>'s. The Wall fell in 1989. On 3 October 1990, the day Germany was officially reunified, East and West Berlin united and joined the Federal Republic as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Berlin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the Glienicke Bridge between <b>West Berlin</b> and Potsdam, the US pilot shot down in 1960 was swapped for the KGB colonel Rudolf Abel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet forces cut road, rail and canal links to <b>West Berlin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-24">24 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After almost eleven months, Soviet authorities reopened road and rail routes into <b>West Berlin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Cooperation broke down as the Cold War began: the western zones introduced the Deutsche Mark in 1948, and the Soviets blockaded <b>West Berlin</b>, answered by the Berlin Airlift.</blockquote><a href="/state/allied-occupied-germany">Allied-occupied Germany</a></li></ul></section>
